// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`format > explain and markdown 1`] = `
"MVVM201 · VIEW OWNS STATE (view-owns-state)
View · error · configurable · also in ESLint

State and effects live in the ViewModel, not in View hooks.

Why
  In MVVM the View has no state: it only reflects the ViewModel's and delegates actions
  to it. With useState/useEffect in the View, logic gets scattered across components
  and can no longer be tested without rendering.

How to fix
  1. Move that state to the ViewModel with this.state(...) and expose it with
     .readonly.
  2. Read it in the View with useBinding(vm.x) and change it with a command.
  3. If it is purely visual state (an open dropdown), you can allow it with
     view.allowHooks in bull.config.ts.

✗ Don't
  │ const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  │ <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>

✓ Do
  │ // viewmodel
  │ readonly #count = this.state(0);
  │ readonly count = this.#count.readonly;
  │ readonly increment = this.command(() => this.#count.update((n) => n + 1));
  │ 
  │ // view
  │ const count = useBinding(vm.count);
  │ <button onClick={vm.increment.execute}>
"
`;

exports[`format > terminal report 1`] = `
"── VIEW IMPORTS MODEL ───────────────────────────── src/view/label.view.tsx:2:1  MVVM100

  The View imports CounterRepository directly from the Model.

  1 │ import { useState } from "react";
  2 │ import { CounterRepository } from "../model/counter.repository";
    │ ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^

  The View only knows its ViewModel. If it talks to the Model directly, presentation
  logic gets tied to the UI: it can no longer be tested without React, and changing
  where the data comes from means touching components. The ViewModel exists precisely
  to translate between both layers.

  How to fix:
    1. Move the use of CounterRepository to LabelViewModel (inject it through the
       constructor).
    2. Expose the data as state/computed and the actions as command.
    3. In the View, use useViewModel(...) and useBinding(...).

  More info: bull explain MVVM100

── VIEW OWNS STATE ─────────────────────────────── src/view/label.view.tsx:5:27  MVVM201

  The View uses useState.

  4 │ export function LabelView(props: { text: string }) {
  5 │   const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
    │                           ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  6 │   return <span>{props.text}</span>;

  In MVVM the View has no state: it only reflects the ViewModel's and delegates
  actions to it. With useState/useEffect in the View, logic gets scattered across
  components and can no longer be tested without rendering.

  How to fix:
    1. Move that state to LabelViewModel with this.state(...) and expose it with
       .readonly.
    2. Read it in the View with useBinding(vm.x) and change it with a command.
    3. If it is purely visual state (an open dropdown), you can allow it with
       view.allowHooks in bull.config.ts.

  More info: bull explain MVVM201

  ✗ MVVM architecture: 2 errors in 1 file · Xms

  Allowed direction:  View ──▶ ViewModel ──▶ Model
  Dependencies that break the direction:
    ✗ View ──▶ Model               ×1    MVVM100
  Other rules:
    ✗ View owns state                      ×1    MVVM201
"
`;
